---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { withBase } from '../site';

const stations = [
  { name: 'Industrial settlements', text: 'Built around mineral deposits and geothermal plants. They keep the heavy machinery of civilisation running.' },
  { name: 'Biological stations', text: 'Aquaculture, algae and food, from organisms native to Thalassa and carefully adapted descendants of Earth species.' },
  { name: 'Research outposts', text: 'Sometimes well funded. Sometimes a handful of scientists and equipment that should have been replaced decades ago.' },
  { name: 'Trading hubs', text: 'Cargo, crew, repairs and rumours. The closest thing Thalassa has to cities.' },
  { name: 'Independent installations', text: 'Private mines, isolated labs, abandoned facilities, and places that left the trade networks on purpose. Some welcome visitors. Some do not.' },
];

const aboard = ['A bridge', 'Engineering', 'Crew quarters', 'A galley', 'Laboratories', 'Medical', 'An observation lounge', 'A hangar', 'Narrow corridors between them all'];
---

<Base
  title="The World"
  description="The setting of THALASSA: an ocean world where humanity lives beneath the waves, most of the charts are blank, and something very large may or may not exist."
  image="/images/concept/ruins.jpg"
>
  <header class="opening">
    <img
      src={withBase('/images/concept/ruins.jpg')}
      alt="Concept art: stone arches and stairs on the seabed, lit from within by cyan bioluminescence."
      width="1000"
      height="857"
    />
    <div class="wrap opening-text">
      <p class="eyebrow">Working sketch / the setting</p>
      <h1>The World</h1>
      <div class="narration">
        <p>There is a world far from Earth.</p>
        <p>A world without continents. Without roads connecting its cities.</p>
        <p>There is only water.</p>
        <p>You have a ship. You have a crew. And beyond your observation windows lies an ocean that does not yet know your name.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="wrap">
    <aside class="notice">
      <p class="label">Status: concept</p>
      <p>
        This is an early sketch of where THALASSA is set and what it is about. It will change as the game does, and
        almost none of it exists in the game yet. <a href={withBase('/devlog/')}>The devlog</a> is the place to see what does.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <section class="chapter">
      <p class="eyebrow">Survey file 01</p>
      <h2>A second ocean</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Nobody came to Thalassa expecting to build a civilisation. It was logged as a resource world: distant, breathable,
          rich in minerals and unusually full of life. Orbital surveys found almost no land. More than ninety-nine percent of
          the surface was water, over mountain ranges, drowned continents and trenches deeper than the first vehicles could reach.
        </p>
        <p class="pull">The planet was beautiful. It was also profoundly inconvenient.</p>
        <p>
          Surface storms made floating installations expensive to keep, so the first permanent settlements went underwater:
          along the continental shelves, in sheltered formations, and near hydrothermal vents where energy and food were easy
          to find. Settlements became towns, and towns became ports and independent communities. Generations passed, and
          children were born who had never stood on solid ground. To them the ocean was not an alien environment. It was home.
        </p>
        <p>They named it with a word borrowed from an ancient Earth language. <em>Thalassa.</em> The sea.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="contour" />

    <section class="chapter">
      <p class="eyebrow">Survey file 02</p>
      <h2>The civilisation beneath</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          No empire rules Thalassa. People are scattered across enormous distances, connected by the ocean and isolated by it
          too. A trip between stations can take hours or much longer, depending on distance, currents, your vessel and what
          you meet on the way.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="stations">
        {
          stations.map((station) => (
            <li>
              <h3>{station.name}</h3>
              <p>{station.text}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Not every unfamiliar station is dangerous, and not every friendly-looking one is entirely honest. But most people
          here are just trying to make a living. Nobody is fighting a grand war for the ocean. They are trying to survive,
          prosper, and understand the planet they live on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="contour" />

    <section class="chapter">
      <p class="eyebrow">Survey file 03</p>
      <h2>We have mapped almost nothing</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          After generations here, the charts still only cover the places people regularly go: shipping corridors, continental
          shelves, known mineral fields. Beyond those, they grow uncertain. Depths are incomplete, sonar records contradict one
          another, some geological structures appear to shift between surveys, and enormous migrating organisms get in the way
          of the mapping.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing forbids exploring the blanks. There is just always something more practical to do. A captain delivering
          medicine has no reason to descend into an unknown trench, and a mining company will not pay to look where nothing is
          confirmed. That is where independent exploration vessels come in.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Survey file 04</p>
        <h2>A home between worlds</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Your ship is a large, independent expedition submarine. It is not a warship, though it can protect itself. It is
            not a liner, though its crew have made it comfortable. It is not just a freighter, though hauling supplies often
            pays the bills.
          </p>
          <p>
            It has a history, and some peculiarities. The crew know which doors need a little extra force to close, which
            coolant pump makes a strange noise on startup, and which observation window has the best view through the
            illuminated shallows.
          </p>
          <p>
            You recruit specialists from the settlements, and over time they become familiar faces, with routines, experience,
            and memories of the expeditions they survived.
          </p>
          <p class="pull">A submarine can be repaired. A lost crew member is not always so easily replaced.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ship-side">
        <figure>
          <img src={withBase('/images/concept/submarine.jpg')} alt="Concept art: a riveted, weathered submarine with lit windows, its lamps cutting through dark water." loading="lazy" width="1000" height="598" />
          <figcaption>Concept art, not the game.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="label">Aboard</p>
        <ul class="aboard">
          {aboard.map((space) => <li>{space}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="contour" />

    <section class="chapter">
      <p class="eyebrow">Survey file 05</p>
      <h2>The ocean is alive</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Reefs stretch across the sunlit shelves, and plant-like colonies rise from the seabed as whole forests. Deeper down,
          life gets stranger. Luminous animals drift through enormous dark spaces, massive filter feeders follow ancient
          migration routes, and predators hunt by pressure, heat, electricity and sound. Some organisms are colonies of
          thousands. Some live for extraordinary lengths of time. Many species have been classified. Countless others have not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Researchers tend to name unfamiliar creatures after Earth animals, not always because the resemblance means anything.
          One common edible species is known across several settlements as the <strong>Sea Chicken</strong>. Its scientific name
          is considerably less convenient, and the nickname has survived generations of taxonomic objections.
        </p>
        <p>Even on another world, people remain people.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="contour" />

    <section class="chapter unresolved">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open file / unresolved</p>
      <h2>The Long Call</h2>
      <svg class="trace" viewBox="0 0 600 60" preserveAspectRatio="none" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M0 30h170c18 0 22-3 34-3s18 6 34 6 16-22 34-22 18 44 40 44 20-34 38-34 14 14 30 14 18-5 34-5h186" />
      </svg>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          In the early decades of deep exploration, hydrophones recorded an unusually powerful low-frequency signal. It was
          filed as geological, because Thalassa makes plenty of strange sounds. But on several occasions, instruments thousands
          of kilometres apart seem to have recorded the same event.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some scientists proposed an enormous organism. Others suggested tectonic resonance, deep currents, or errors in the
          early sensors. One research vessel recorded what sounded like several immense tones at once. None of it proved
          anything, and most people moved on with their lives.
        </p>
        <p>Occasionally, deep listening stations still record it. Sometimes decades pass between events. The source is unknown.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter unresolved viator">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Open file / unconfirmed</p>
        <h2>The Viator</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            One of the oldest stories among deep-water crews is of the Viator, from an old Earth word for <em>traveller</em>:
            an organism of extraordinary size, moving slowly through the deepest water. Some accounts describe a surface
            mistaken for the seabed. Others describe a single eye so large a submarine could cross it without realising what it
            was looking at.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is no reliable evidence it is hostile. The few credible accounts suggest it takes remarkably little interest
            in ships. It may be one of a population. It may be exhausted sailors and misread geology. The most controversial
            theory says it was here long before humanity arrived, and perhaps long before humanity existed.
          </p>
          <p class="pull">
            On Thalassa, the difference between mythology and biology is sometimes a matter of obtaining a sufficiently good photograph.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <img src={withBase('/images/concept/leviathan.jpg')} alt="Concept art: a whale-like creature speckled with glowing points, drifting beneath the surface above a school of fish." loading="lazy" width="1000" height="620" />
        <figcaption>Concept art. Not a sufficiently good photograph.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <hr class="contour" />

    <section class="chapter">
      <p class="eyebrow">Ship's log</p>
      <h2>No chosen one. No imminent apocalypse.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          No prophecy predicts your arrival, and no catastrophe waits on you finishing a list of missions. You command an
          exploration vessel. Nothing more, and that is enough.
        </p>
        <p>
          You might haul supplies between stations, investigate abandoned facilities, photograph unknown species, salvage
          wrecks, or help an isolated settlement with a practical problem. You might become a respected explorer, or simply a
          competent captain who looks after a ship and crew.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mysteries of the deep can connect through what you discover, but you will never get a mission that says
          <em>Find the Viator: 0/1</em>. Someone records a peculiar signal, and nobody thinks much of it. Several expeditions
          later, someone recognises the same sound. Much later, deep inside an unexplored trench, you realise what you have
          been listening to. Or you never do.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some expeditions are peaceful scientific journeys. Others are desperate struggles to bring a damaged submarine home.
          And some evenings are just beautiful water, the crew talking, and unfamiliar creatures drifting past the window.
        </p>
        <p class="pull">The ocean existed before you arrived. It will continue to exist when you leave.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter">
      <p class="eyebrow">Ship's log</p>
      <h2>Humanity travels with us</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          People still need ordinary things out here: a decent meal, a comfortable bed, someone to complain to when the coffee
          machine breaks. Crews form habits and friendships, and argue over whose turn it is to clean the galley. The ship's AI
          can be strictly professional or somewhat more expressive, but it is equally competent either way, and an emergency is
          never a joke.
        </p>
        <p>
          The humour comes from the people, not from a universe that refuses to take itself seriously. An engineer makes a dry
          comment about the captain's piloting. A cook insists an unfamiliar animal tastes exactly like chicken. These moments
          make the ship feel inhabited, and they make the silence of the abyss considerably more unsettling.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="contour" />

    <section class="chapter closing">
      <p class="eyebrow">What this is really about</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Thalassa does not exist to satisfy human expectations. A creature that destroys a submarine may be defending its
          territory. A vast shadow beneath the window may be no danger at all. The ocean is neither kind nor cruel, and the
          real challenge is understanding enough to share it, and perhaps learning when to leave something undisturbed.
        </p>
        <p>
          At heart this is a game about curiosity, about the people you live and work with in a small and isolated place,
          about looking after the machinery that keeps you alive, and about the peculiar mix of courage, foolishness and
          optimism it takes to go somewhere too vast to ever fully understand.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="final">We are not here to conquer it.<br />We are here to explore.</p>
      <p class="sign"><em>Beep. Bloop.</em></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .opening {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    align-items: end;
    min-height: min(74vh, 660px);
    overflow: hidden;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--panel-edge);
  }
  .opening > img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% 55%;
  }
  .opening::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(to top, var(--abyss) 8%, rgba(6, 19, 29, 0.72) 55%, rgba(6, 19, 29, 0.25));
  }
  .opening-text { position: relative; z-index: 1; padding-block: 3rem 2.5rem; }
  h1 { margin: 0 0 1.2rem; font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 4.4rem); font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: 0.06em; }
  .narration { max-width: 34rem; font-style: italic; font-size: 1.1em; }
  .narration p { margin: 0 0 0.6rem; }
  .narration p:last-child { margin: 1rem 0 0; color: var(--amber); }

  .notice {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    background: var(--panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--panel-edge);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--amber);
  }
  .notice p { margin: 0; }
  .notice .label { color: var(--amber); margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }

  .chapter { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem); }
  h2 { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 1.3rem + 1.4vw, 2.4rem); margin: 0 0 1.2rem; }
  .pull { font-family: var(--display); font-size: 1.2em; line-height: 1.35; color: var(--amber); }

  .stations {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 250px), 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem 2rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .stations li { border-top: 1px solid var(--sonar); padding-top: 0.8rem; }
  .stations h3 { margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-size: 1.1rem; }
  .stations p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95em; }

  .split, .viator { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 2fr); gap: 2rem 3.5rem; align-items: start; }
  figure { margin: 0; }
  figure img { width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--panel-edge); }
  figcaption { margin-top: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .ship-side > .label { margin: 1.8rem 0 0.6rem; }
  .aboard { margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.98em; }
  .aboard li { padding-block: 0.3rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--panel-edge); }

  .unresolved .eyebrow { color: var(--coral); }
  .trace { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: var(--measure); height: 60px; margin-bottom: 1.2rem; fill: none; stroke: var(--sonar); stroke-width: 1.2; vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke; }
  .trace path { vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke; }

  .final { margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem; font-family: var(--display); font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.2rem + 1.5vw, 2.4rem); line-height: 1.25; }
  .sign { margin: 0; color: var(--amber); font-size: 1.2em; }

  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .split, .viator { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
